Drive the Marvell network coprocessor on Sophos XGS appliances.

On these boards every front port belongs to a Marvell coprocessor behind a PCIe endpoint, and
the host cannot reach a single one of them until that coprocessor has been brought up and
talked to. Two families are covered and they are not the same silicon.

On ARMADA boards - the XGS 136 and its siblings - the coprocessor comes out of power-on held
in reset by a USB-to-SPI bridge whose factory pin state is "held". The vendor firmware releases
it during its own startup. Without that step the coprocessor never boots, its serial console
never says a word, and the appliance has no front ports at all. What this package installs is
that release, as an early boot hook.

On OCTEON TX boards - the XGS 3300 - the coprocessor boots on its own and the work is the
protocol: an endpoint handshake, a facility mailbox, and the control plane that raises a front
port and reads its link back.

On these boards every front port belongs to a Marvell CN913x coprocessor behind a PCIe
endpoint, and that coprocessor comes out of power-on held in reset by a USB-to-SPI bridge whose
factory pin state is "held". The vendor firmware releases it during its own startup. Without
that step the coprocessor never boots, its serial console never says a word, and the appliance
has no network interfaces at all.

This plugin performs that release on every boot, before anything expects interfaces to exist.
The pin values are per board model and are read from the bridge's own EEPROM, so it acts only
on hardware it recognises and does nothing at all on anything else.

It also ships the kernel module that completes the handshake the coprocessor waits for, as
source, to be built and loaded deliberately rather than automatically.

